>
  <RecipeSchema {...recipe} datePublished="2024-06-01" dateModified="2026-07-18" url={canonical} />
  <h1>How to Make Beef Jerky</h1>

  <div class="answer-box">
    <p>Making beef jerky has four steps: <strong>slice</strong> lean beef 1/4 inch thin, <strong>marinate</strong> 8–24 hours in soy sauce and spices, <strong>dry</strong> at 160°F for 4–6 hours (dehydrator) or 175°F for 3–5 hours (oven), then <strong>test</strong> with the bend test. Cool completely before storing. The whole process is simple — the hardest part is waiting.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Step 1: Choose the Right Meat</h2>
  <p>The best beef for jerky is <strong>eye of round</strong> or <strong>top round</strong> — lean, affordable, and easy to slice. Buy a single muscle roast, not pre-cut steaks. Trim every visible strip of fat before slicing — fat goes rancid and shortens shelf life dramatically.</p>
  <p>See our complete <a href="/best-meat-for-beef-jerky/">guide to the best meat for beef jerky</a> for a full cut comparison.</p>

  <img src="/images/beef-jerky-slicing.webp" width="1200" height="896" alt="Lean beef being sliced paper-thin against the grain on a dark cutting board for jerky making" style="width:100%;border-radius:8px;margin:1.5rem 0;"  loading="eager" fetchpriority="high"/>

  <h2>Step 2: Slice the Beef</h2>
  <p>Freeze the roast for 1–2 hours until firm but not solid. This makes hand-slicing much easier. Slice <strong>1/4 inch thick</strong>:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Against the grain</strong> = chewy, standard jerky texture (recommended)</li>
    <li><strong>With the grain</strong> = stringy, tender, pull-apart texture</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Step 3: Make the Marinade</h2>
  <div class="recipe-section">
    <p><strong>Classic Homemade Beef Jerky Marinade (for 2 lbs beef):</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>1/3 cup soy sauce</li>
      <li>2 tablespoons Worcestershire sauce</li>
      <li>1 teaspoon garlic powder</li>
      <li>1 teaspoon onion powder</li>
      <li>1 teaspoon black pepper</li>
      <li>1/2 teaspoon smoked paprika</li>
      <li>Optional: 1/4 tsp Prague Powder No. 1 for longer shelf life</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <p>Combine everything in a zip-lock bag, add the beef strips, remove excess air, seal, and refrigerate 8–24 hours. For flavor variations, see our <a href="/beef-jerky-marinade/">complete marinade guide</a> — or skip the liquid entirely with the <a href="/beef-jerky-dry-rub/">best dry rub recipe for beef jerky</a>.</p>

  <h2>Step 4: Dry the Beef Jerky</h2>
  <p>Remove strips from the marinade and <strong>pat completely dry</strong> with paper towels. Then choose your drying method:</p>

  <table class="data-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Method</th><th>Temp</th><th>Time</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Food dehydrator</td><td>160°F</td><td>4–6 hrs</td><td>Best, most consistent</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Oven</td><td>175°F</td><td>3–5 hrs</td><td>Prop door open 1 inch</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Smoker</td><td>160–175°F</td><td>4–6 hrs</td><td>Best flavor</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Air fryer</td><td>160°F</td><td>2–4 hrs</td><td>Fastest; small batches only</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>Working on a pellet grill? It's the easiest smoker to hold at jerky temperature — our <a href="/smoked-beef-jerky/#traeger-pellet-grill">Traeger and pellet grill beef jerky guide</a> covers exact settings, timing, and which pellets to use.</p>

  <img src="/images/beef-jerky-done.webp" width="1200" height="896" alt="Finished homemade beef jerky strips in rich dark mahogany color ready to eat" style="width:100%;border-radius:8px;margin:1.5rem 0;" loading="lazy"  decoding="async"/>

  <h2>Step 5: Test for Doneness</h2>
  <p>The <strong>bend test</strong> is the most reliable doneness check. Remove a strip from the heat, cool 2–3 minutes, then bend it sharply at 90°:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>✅ <strong>Done:</strong> Cracks and bends; feels firm and dry; no moist areas</li>
    <li>❌ <strong>Not done:</strong> Bends without cracking; feels rubbery or soft</li>
    <li>⚠️ <strong>Overdone:</strong> Snaps in two; very hard and brittle</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Step 6: Cool and Store</h2>
  <p>Spread finished jerky on a wire rack and cool to room temperature — at least 1 hour. Never seal hot jerky in a container; condensation will make it soggy and accelerate mold.</p>
  <p>Store in an airtight zip-lock bag or container: 1–2 weeks at room temperature, 1 month refrigerated, 6 months frozen. See our full <a href="/guides/how-long-does-beef-jerky-last/">beef jerky storage guide</a> for shelf life details, and the <a href="/guides/is-beef-jerky-healthy/">homemade beef jerky nutrition guide</a> for what a batch actually delivers per serving.</p>

  <h2>How to Make Beef Jerky More Tender</h2>
  <p>For tender beef jerky:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Slice <strong>with the grain</strong> (not against it) — long fibers produce a pull-apart, tender chew</li>
    <li>Slice slightly thicker: <strong>3/8 inch</strong> instead of 1/4 inch</li>
    <li>Add <strong>1/4 cup pineapple juice</strong> to the marinade — bromelain naturally tenderizes beef</li>
    <li>Pull the jerky <strong>just as it passes the bend test</strong> — not after it starts getting hard</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>How to Make Beef Jerky Softer After Drying</h2>
  <p>If your jerky came out too hard: seal strips in a zip-lock bag with 1 tablespoon of water, squeeze out air, and let sit 2–4 hours at room temperature. The jerky will reabsorb moisture and soften. Use softened jerky within 24–48 hours — it won't have the shelf life of properly dried jerky.</p>

  <h2>How to Make Crispy Beef Jerky</h2>
  <p>For crispy jerky: slice against the grain at <strong>1/8 inch</strong> (very thin), skip sugary ingredients in the marinade, and dehydrate 1–2 hours longer than standard. The result is a chip-like crunch rather than a chew. Great for crushing into seasoning or trail mix.</p>
